Indeed. One could say the same about chess. You buy the board: you buy
the pieces: you buy a copy of the rules: but you DO NOT buy the right
to change the rules of the game and still call it Chess. You might
play against opponents who allow you to "take back" a bad move if you
make one: this does not make doing so any the more legal, according to
the rules of the game. If you want to solve a chess problem in a
newspaper, you have to use the existing rules.

Likewise, using backup savefiles (which, in effect, is EXACTLY the
same as taking back a move in chess - i.e. restoring the game to a
situation before the mistake you made) is cheating in Nethack, and
just because you do it does not mean that it is not cheating. Nobody
can stop you, so long as you're only cheating yourself (just as, if
you're playing a chess game against yourself, playing both white and
black moves, you can feel free to take back moves and replace them
with better moves at your leisure), but the moment it starts to
impinge on other people - i.e. you boast about your "victories" that
are achieved by cheating, or attempt (a) to teach people to cheat, or
(b) to falsely tell them that certain actions are not cheating when,
in fact, they are - then we, the people who read your false public
pronouncements, have the right to complain.

